Consider the following example:

```c++
struct ExpensiveObject {
  int large_array[1024 * 1024]; // Simulates a large object
  ExpensiveObject() { /* initialization */ }
  ExpensiveObject(const ExpensiveObject& other) { /* expensive copy */ }
  ExpensiveObject(ExpensiveObject&& other) noexcept { /* move constructor */ }
};

ExpensiveObject createExpensiveObject() {
  ExpensiveObject obj;
  // ... some work...
  return obj; // RVO or NRVO can eliminate copy/move here
}

int main() {
  ExpensiveObject myObject = createExpensiveObject(); // myObject is created directly in place
  return 0;
}
```

The compiler might perform RVO by constructing `myObject` directly in `main()`'s stack space, skipping the creation of a temporary object in `createExpensiveObject()` and any subsequent copy or move.

